\section{Introduction}

In this study we will investigate how to use R packages such as mice and miclust to apply statistical tools for missing data. Our goal is to generate clusters to analyze and visualize our dataset. This dataset is a subset of the variables studied in the original paper by Basagaña et al.CITA AQUI 


\section{Analysis}
\subsection{Description of missingness}
<<libraries, include=FALSE>>=
### libraries related to knit:
library(knitr)
library(xtable)
@

<<data, include=FALSE>>=
load("copd_redux.RData")
@

<<missingness, echo=TRUE>>=

n_participants <- nrow(copd)
n_variables <- ncol(copd)
missing_per_variable <- colMeans(is.na(copd)) * 100

min_missing <- sprintf("%.1f", min(missing_per_variable))
max_missing <- sprintf("%.1f", max(missing_per_variable))

pct_variables_lt5 <- sprintf("%.1f", mean(missing_per_variable < 5) * 100) # mean
# calcula la proporció de true
# equivalent: AA <- sprintf("%.1f", sum((missing_per_variable < 5))/n_variables * 100)
# true=1, false=0
 
pct_complete_cases <- sprintf("%.1f", mean(complete.cases(copd)) * 100)
pct_missing_total <- sprintf("%.1f", mean(is.na(as.matrix(copd))) * 100)

total_cells <- prettyNum(n_participants * n_variables, big.mark=",") # n_participants
                                                    # * n_variables = total_cells
@

The subset used here included \Sexpr{n_participants} participants and \Sexpr{n_variables} variables. The range of missing data in the different variables went from \Sexpr{min_missing}\% to \Sexpr{max_missing}\%, with \Sexpr{pct_variables_lt5}\% of the variables having less than 5\% of the data missing. Only \Sexpr{pct_complete_cases}\% of the participants had complete data on all \Sexpr{n_variables} variables. Overall, \Sexpr{pct_missing_total}\% of the information was missing; that is, \Sexpr{pct_missing_total}\% of the total of
\Sexpr{total_cells} cells (\Sexpr{n_variables} x \Sexpr{n_participants} = \Sexpr{total_cells}) had missing data.

\subsection{Multiple imputation}
Apply multiple imputation to copd data using 100 imputations. Explain in a reasoned
manner how you decided to set the values of the arguments method and predictorMatrix
when using the mice() function. Show your code

<<libraries2, include=FALSE>>=
library(mice)
@

<<multiple_imputation, echo=TRUE, cache=TRUE>>=

ini <- mice(copd, maxit = 0)

meth <- ini$method
pred <- ini$predictorMatrix

meth["id"] <- "" # do not impute "id"
pred["id", ] <- 0 # we don't want "id" to be imputed
pred[, "id"] <- 0 # we don't want "id" to be predictor

# Fem les 100 imputacions:
imp <- mice(copd, method = meth, predictorMatrix = pred, m = 100,
            printFlag = FALSE, seed = 123)
@

Wekept the same methodology and the prediction matrix, with the exception of id as it isn't relevant for this process.
The variable 'm' encodes de number of imputations, 'printFlag' is used to show the current imputation although it's not important for the results,
and the 'seed' is set up for replicability. 


\subsection{Integration of multiple imputation in cluster analysis}

Reproduce the original analysis in the paper using the miclust package to integrate multiple
imputation in the cluster analysis. Apply the proposed framework using the same setting
than in the original analysis.

To do that, follow these steps:
<<libraries3, include=FALSE>>=
library(miclust)
@

1. Read the help of the getdata() function in the miclust package. Then, accordingly,
create a list of data.frames and pass it to the getdata() function. Give a detailed
explanation of the data processing you did to get that list. Show your code.

<<integration, echo=TRUE, cache=TRUE>>=

resultat_mice <- complete(imp, action = "all", include = TRUE)
# action = "all" ja em posa les dades incials com a primer element

categoriques <- c(
  "sin11_v1", "iam_chv1", "sin15_v1", "sin7_v1", "sin8_v1",
  "cccs1_v0", "vtit_catv1", "urg_ambv0", "sin5_v1",
  "sin6_v1", "graudisn_v1"
)

resultat_mice <- lapply(resultat_mice, function(d) {
  d$id <- NULL
  d[categoriques] <- lapply(d[categoriques], function(x)
    as.numeric(as.character(x)))
  d
})

miclustdata <- getdata(resultat_mice) # adapta les dades al format de miclust
@

As 'getdata()' necessitates a list of dataframes, where the first
element has the initial data and the imputations are stored in the subsequent elements,
we call the 'complete' function that returns the needed information when using
"action=all" and "include=TRUE". Furthermore, we must adapt the categorical
variables to numerical values. Thus we define a function that transforms the values stored
in text into numbers and using the 'lapply()' function, we reach our goal: the list of dataframes that 'getdata()' needs.


2. Use the miclust() function to perform the integration of missing data in cluster
analysis using the same settings as in the original analysis. Show your code and
explain how and why you set all arguments in the miclust() function.

<<integration2, echo=TRUE, cache=TRUE>>=
# Segon apartat:  
copd_miclustdata <- miclust(data = miclustdata,search = "backward",
                            ks = 2:3,
                            distance = "manhattan",
                            centpos = "means",
                            initcl = "hc",
                            verbose = TRUE,
                            seed = 123)

@


The chosen elements have been: 'backward', because we were instructed to
during class; 'ks = 2:3', as the paper itself led us to believe that a high
number of clusters would lead to inaccurate  results; 'manhattan', the default
option; 'means', as we are using the k-means algorithm; 'hc', we decided on 
a jerarquical inicialitzation over a random one for better results;
'verbose = TRUE', so show the progress of this long process; 'seed=123' for replicability.


\section{Results}
Using plot() and summary() methods in the miclust package, provide plots1 and tables
for the results similar than those shown in the original paper. Also, show a barplot for the
percentage of times each variable is selected among the imputed data sets, in decreasing
order2. Explain how you set all arguments in all functions and methods you use. Show
your code.


<<plot1, echo=TRUE, fig.cap="Number of clusters frequency", fig.width=6, fig.height=3, fig.pos = 'H'>>=
plot(copd_miclustdata, metric="nclfreq", col.nclfreq = "blue")
@

<<plot2, echo=TRUE, fig.cap="CritCF value versus number of clusters", fig.width=6, fig.height=6, fig.pos = 'H'>>=
plot(copd_miclustdata, metric="critcf", col.critcf = "orange")
@

<<plot3, echo=TRUE, fig.cap="Frequency of the number of times each variable being selected furing multiple imputation", fig.width=6, fig.height=4, fig.pos = 'H'>>=
plot(copd_miclustdata, metric="nvarfreq", col.nvarfreq = "forestgreen")
@

<<plot4, echo=TRUE, fig.cap="Frequency of each variable being selected during multiple imputation", fig.width=6, fig.height=4, fig.pos = 'H'>>=
plot(copd_miclustdata, metric="varsel", col.varsel = "gray")
@

We only needed to define the colors to show the plots.


<<mytable1, echo=TRUE, results = 'asis'>>=

res2 <- summary(copd_miclustdata, k = 2, quantilevars = 0.5)
res3 <- summary(copd_miclustdata, k = 3, quantilevars = 0.5)

resum_prob <- rbind(res2$allocationprobabilities, res3$allocationprobabilities)

rownames(resum_prob) <- c("k = 2, Cluster 1",
                          "k = 2, Cluster 2",
                          "k = 3, Cluster 1",
                          "k = 3, Cluster 2",
                          "k = 3, Cluster 3")

modtable <- xtable(resum_prob,
                   digits = c(0, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3),
                   caption = "Distribution of the Frequencies of Assignment
                   (Proportions) for Each Cluster (for k = 2 and k = 3)",
                   label = "tab:frequency")
# cosmetics for table head (LaTeX slash (\) must be doubled inside a R chunk):
colnames(modtable) <- c("\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Minimum}",
                        "\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf First Quartile}",
                        "\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Median}",
                        "\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Third Quartile}",
                        "\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Maximum}")

# format and print the table:
print(modtable,
      table.placement = "ht",
      size = "footnotesize",
      include.rownames = TRUE,
      include.colnames = TRUE,
      floating = TRUE,                 # whether \begin{table}
      # should be created (TRUE) or not (FALSE)
      # important to treat content
      # of columns as latex function
      sanitize.text.function = force) 
@

Answer: To create the first table we use the 'summary()' function for the
differnet k-values, and using a template provided by the professor, we show the
desired results.

<<mytable2, echo=TRUE, results = 'asis'>>=

descr <- as.data.frame(res2$summarybycluster)

taula3 <- data.frame(Variable = gsub("_", "\\\\_", rownames(descr)),
                     Missing = as.numeric(descr[["%miss."]]),
                     Cluster1 = as.numeric(descr[["mean (cl.1)"]]),
                     Cluster2 = as.numeric(descr[["mean (cl.2)"]]),
                     check.names = FALSE
)

n <- res2$clusterssize[, "50%"] # Valor del nombre medià dels clusters

modtable <- xtable(taula3,
                   digits = c(0, 0, 2, 2, 2),
                   caption = "Description of the Variables (Mean Values) by Cluster",
                   label = "tab:descr")
# cosmetics for table head (LaTeX slash (\) must be doubled inside a R chunk):
colnames(modtable) <- c("\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Variable}",
                        "\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf \\% of Subjects With Missing Values}",
                        paste0("\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Cluster 1 (n = ", n[1], ")}"), 
                        #paste0 per a ajuntar strings
                        paste0("\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Cluster 2 (n = ", n[2], ")}"))

# format and print the table:
print(modtable,
      table.placement = "ht",
      size = "footnotesize",
      include.rownames = FALSE,
      include.colnames = TRUE,
      # whether \begin{table} should be created (TRUE) or not (FALSE)
      floating = TRUE,                 
      # important to treat content of columns as latex function
      sanitize.text.function = force)  

@

We haven't included the 'F values' in the second table, nor used
the unprocessed data for the average-value analisys, but we have decided to
show other results from the 'summary()' function, those we considered relevant
to the imputated data. To do this, an auxiliary dataframa of the 'summary' was
needed. Furthermore, we obtained the mean valur of the clusters just like the 
original paper did.

\section{Report}

The final analysis divided the dataset in \textbf{2 clusters}. We can see in
Figure~\ref{fig:plot1} that k=2 (having 2 clusters) is chosen every time, thus we can
conclude without uncertainty that we can divide the dataset in two clusters.
Table~\ref{tab:descr} details the size of each of these clusters: n=186 for the first
cluster and n=156 for the second one.

The relabeling of the clusters over multiple imputations (the same cluster being
labelled differently in different imputations) is handled internally by the
\textbf{miclust} package, as detailed in its documentation.

Figure~\ref{fig:plot3} shows how many variables were selected across the
imputations: an average of 16.5, with a median of 17, standard deviation of
0.8 and an interquartile range of 16-17. Thus, the final subset contains 17
variables and the uncertaintity over this choice is low. In 
Figure~\ref{fig:plot4} shows us the selection frequency for each variable: the
most selected include activityv1, fvcp_postv1, ic_prepv1, rv_prepv1, sin15_v1,
vc_prepv1, po2_v1, vas1_v1, index_barthel2v1 and cpcr_mg_l_mediav1. Table~{tab:descr}
lists the variables used in the descriptive cluster.

In Figure~\ref{fig:mytable1}, we can see that, for every cluster with (k=2) and (k=3), at least
three-quarters of its instances are assigned to the same cluster across all imputations. 
Therefore, assuming that the missing data follow the proposed distributions, at least 75\% 
of all instances don't have uncertainty.




\section{Use of artificial intelligence}

Artificial Intelligence, more accurately Large Language Models (LLMs), have been
used to revise and enhance the code displayed in this paper. Here is the exact
prompt and answer of the one time they were used:

\textbf{Prompt:} 

\begin{verbatim}
Tinc aquest codi en R:
resultat_mice<-complete(imp, action="all", include=TRUE)
categorics_amb_ordre <- c("sin11_v1", "iam_chv1", "sin15_v1", "sin7_v1", "sin8_v1","cccs1_v0", "vtit_catv1", "urg_ambv0", "sin5_v1", "sin6_v1")
categorics_sense_ordre <- c("graudisn_v1")
for (i in 1:(M+1))
{
  for (j in categorics_amb_ordre)
  {
    resultat_mice[[i]][[j]] <- as.numeric(levels(resultat_mice[[i]][[j]]))[resultat_mice[[i]][[j]]]
  }
  for (j in categorics_sense_ordre)
  {
    resultat_mice[[i]][[j]] <- as.numeric(levels(resultat_mice[[i]][[j]]))[resultat_mice[[i]][[j]]]
  }
}
miclustdata<-getdata(resultat_mice)


Saps si es pot optimitzar i simplificar? També voldria eliminar la columna id de cada conjunt de dades imputat abans de crear miclustdata.
\end{verbatim}



\textbf{Answer:}

\begin{verbatim}
Sí. En el teu codi original hi ha dues simplificacions clares:

1. categorics_amb_ordre i categorics_sense_ordre reben exactament el mateix tractament, per tant es poden unir en un únic vector.
2. Es poden evitar els for utilitzant lapply(), cosa que fa el codi més compacte i independent de M.
3. Pots eliminar la columna id dins del mateix procés.

Una possible versió simplificada seria:


resultat_mice <- complete(imp, action = "all", include = TRUE)


categoriques <- c(
  "sin11_v1", "iam_chv1", "sin15_v1", "sin7_v1", "sin8_v1",
  "cccs1_v0", "vtit_catv1", "urg_ambv0", "sin5_v1",
  "sin6_v1", "graudisn_v1"
)

resultat_mice <- lapply(resultat_mice, function(d) {

  # Eliminar la variable identificadora
  d$id <- NULL

  # Convertir factors a numèrics
  d[categoriques] <- lapply(
    d[categoriques],
    function(x) as.numeric(as.character(x))
  )

  d
})

miclustdata <- getdata(resultat_mice)


Aquesta versió manté la mateixa funcionalitat sempre que les variables categòriques tinguin nivells numèrics (per exemple "0", "1", "2", ...), elimina la variable id i evita haver de controlar explícitament el nombre d'imputacions M.
\end{verbatim}


